  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nNorthwesterly upper flow is forecast to be overhead Saturday \nmorning. Ensemble guidance indicates the presence of a shortwave\ntrough embedded in the flow that will traverse the forecast \nregion Saturday. However, it is unclear how strong this system \nwill be once it enters the area. When looking at LREF \ncontributors for the 25 November 00Z run time, around 50-60% of \nGEFS and ENS members show a modestly deep trough and associated \njet streak along the trailing edge that could be upwards of 70 \nkts. On the other hand, the remaining ENS and GEFS members, \nalong with most of the GEPS members show this wave as a shallow \ndisturbance. The solution with a deeper shortwave would express \nitself as a post-frontal mixed precipitation event Saturday as a\nstrong surface low pulls a moist airmass southward into the \narea. NBM guidance shows temperatures falling below freezing \nsometime between the mid evening and nighttime hours, which is \nwhen wintry precipitation would become more likely. When looking\nat the LREF Grand Ensemble for 24 hr snowfall, much of the area\nhas a 20-40% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of snowfall \nSaturday, with the highest of these probabilities in Western \nYuma and Kit Carson Counties in Colorado. Even so, about 25% of \nthe LREF ensemble members show the shallow disturbance, which \ncorrespond only to a 40% chance or less for any precipitation \noccurring at all. The favored solution at this time is the \ndeeper shortwave with mixed precipitation, at around 40-50%. \nStill, confidence in a Winter Weather Advisory being needed is \nlow (10-15%), as even the 95th percentile from the LREF Grand \nEnsemble shows 2 inches of accumulation or less. \n\nEnsembles show northwesterly flow remaining overhead Sunday, \nwith the embedded shortwave trough exiting to the east. As this \noccurs, surface flow is favored to be slowing a bit, but remain \nout of the north to northwest for the majority of the morning \nand afternoon. NBM guidance suggests a 75% chance or greater for\nmost areas in the forecast region to be at or below the lower \n50s for high temperatures. This estimate can vary a bit though \ndepending on how Saturday's event plays out. If colder \ntemperatures and snowfall can occur Saturday, the lower end \nwould likely be experienced. However, temperatures in the 50s \ncould be supported if the warmer, drier solution takes place. \nLooking at 24 hr precipitation guidance, dry conditions are \nexpected throughout the day Sunday. \n\nBy Monday morning, upper flow is forecast to shift to zonal \n(west to east) and remain through the end of the forecast \nperiod. Model guidance shows some shortwave troughs embedded in \nthis flow that could impact the region Monday and Wednesday, \nthough PoPs (probabilities for precipitation) are below 10% \nthrough Wednesday afternoon. There is a portion of LREF \nsolutions (15% total) that show a 20-40% chance of precipitation\noccurring Monday in association with a deeper shortwave, but \ndry conditions are favored otherwise. Current NBM guidance \nsupports high temperatures in the upper 40s or higher during \nthis period, with some higher end scenarios in the 60s Tuesday \nand Wednesday.",
